1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
It's night, and a police officer

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,700
is standing at your car window,

3
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,700
holding your phone.

4
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,320
Everything you are is inside it.

5
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:08,500
And he wants it open.

6
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,639
He can press it to your thumb.

7
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:11,440
He can hold

8
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:12,440
it up to your face.

9
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,526
The one thing he may not be able to force open —

10
00:00:16,527 --> 00:00:17,948
is what's inside your head.

11
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:19,780
This is the story of a question

12
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:22,180
the Supreme Court keeps refusing to answer:

13
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,140
can the police force you to unlock your phone?

14
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:26,800
And why the answer can turn

15
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,209
on one strange detail —

16
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:30,631
whether you used your face,

17
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,940
your thumb, or a passcode you keep

18
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:33,887
only in your mind.

19
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,711
Start with what's actually in your pocket.

20
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,426
Not a phone —

21
00:00:38,427 --> 00:00:39,327
your whole life.

22
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:40,920
Every place you've been,

23
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:42,120
mapped by the minute.

24
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,168
Every message you've sent,

25
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:45,936
every photo you've taken,

26
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,180
who you love, what you fear,

27
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:50,740
what you searched at three in the morning.

28
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,752
There has never been a record
of a human being this complete.

29
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:55,640
And it fits in one hand.

30
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,740
Think about who else knows all

31
00:00:57,740 --> 00:00:58,640
of that.

32
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:00,003
Not your closest friend.

33
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,140
Not your doctor.

34
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:03,860
Not even the person you share a bed with.

35
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:05,680
Your phone has sat in rooms

36
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:06,760
no one else was in.

37
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:11,657
It knows what you look at when you can't
sleep, the things you typed and deleted,

38
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,606
the person you almost called.

39
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:15,680
No human being alive knows

40
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,733
you the way that glowing rectangle does

41
00:01:18,313 --> 00:01:18,613
—

42
00:01:18,614 --> 00:01:20,509
and it never forgets a single thing.

43
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:25,260
That is what's really sitting in the
officer's hand. And this is not some rare,

44
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:26,480
dramatic event.

45
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:30,260
Somewhere in this country, a
driver is pulled over every few

46
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,960
seconds. Almost all of those stops end quietly,

47
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:35,280
forgotten by morning.

48
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,540
But any one of them can end

49
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:38,220
with an open hand and two flat

50
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:39,140
words:

51
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:40,080
unlock it.

52
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:41,740
The one thing that holds your entire

53
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:43,400
life can be demanded in the most

54
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:44,821
ordinary moment there is.

55
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,649
The Supreme Court understood this.

56
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:49,320
In 2014, in a case called Riley

57
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,652
versus California, the justices ruled —

58
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:53,080
unanimously —

59
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:55,820
that a phone is not like anything

60
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:56,720
else you carry.

61
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,060
Before the police can search what's inside

62
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:00,780
it, even after an arrest,

63
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:02,879
they generally need a warrant.

64
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,420
Chief Justice Roberts put it in four plain words:

65
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:06,520
get a warrant.

66
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:08,056
So that's settled, right?

67
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:09,353
They need a warrant.

68
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:10,300
You're protected.

69
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,700
But here is the trapdoor almost everyone

70
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:13,600
falls through.

71
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:16,928
A warrant gives the police
permission to look inside your phone.

72
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:19,140
It does not answer a completely different

73
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:20,040
question —

74
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:23,256
one that lives in a completely
different part of the Constitution.

75
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:27,510
Because a warrant to search your phone
is worthless if they can't get into it.

76
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:28,480
And a modern phone,

77
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,160
locked, is a very good safe.

78
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,160
So the real fight was never
really about searching.

79
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:34,300
It was about this:

80
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,588
can the government force you —

81
00:02:37,589 --> 00:02:38,206
you —

82
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:39,260
to open it yourself?

83
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:40,994
And the moment you ask that,

84
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:43,679
you leave the Fourth Amendment behind —

85
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,199
the one about searches —

86
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:46,674
and you walk into the Fifth.

87
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,540
The one you already know from every movie:

88
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:52,277
you cannot be forced to be
a witness against yourself.

89
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:55,040
But the Fifth Amendment has a strange edge to it.

90
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,120
It doesn't protect everything the
government can pull out of you.

91
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:00,300
It only protects what the courts call

92
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,784
testimonial —

93
00:03:02,785 --> 00:03:05,500
words, or acts, that reveal
the contents of your mind.

94
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:06,860
What you know.

95
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:08,225
What you're thinking.

96
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:12,940
That distinction is about to decide whether
your phone is a fortress or an open door.

97
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:17,320
The courts have a picture for this
that's almost two hundred years

98
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:20,440
old. Imagine the police have a warrant

99
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:22,000
to open a safe.

100
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,120
They can force you to hand over

101
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:24,700
the key.

102
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:27,320
A key is just a physical object;

103
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:29,860
giving it up says nothing about what's

104
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:30,740
in your head.

105
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:33,280
But they cannot force you to tell

106
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,350
them the combination —

107
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:37,422
because the combination lives in your
mind, and speaking it is testimony.

108
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:39,090
Now put your phone in that picture.

109
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:40,740
A passcode is a combination.

110
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:42,960
It's a string of numbers that exists

111
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:44,592
nowhere but inside your memory.

112
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:46,320
Force you to enter it,

113
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:47,658
many courts have said,

114
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:49,300
and you are being made to reveal

115
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:50,668
the contents of your mind.

116
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:52,932
So a passcode may be protected.

117
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:54,360
But your face?

118
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:55,580
Your thumb?

119
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:57,488
Those aren't in your mind.

120
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,085
They're on your body.

121
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:00,840
And the law treats your body very

122
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:01,740
differently.

123
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,800
The police can already take
your fingerprints at booking.

124
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,296
They can draw your blood.

125
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:08,152
They can stand you in a lineup.

126
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:10,545
None of that is testimony —

127
00:04:10,546 --> 00:04:12,180
it reveals nothing you know.

128
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:14,560
It's just physical evidence you happen

129
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:15,460
to be made of.

130
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,820
Which leads somewhere most
people never see coming.

131
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,949
In 2024, a federal appeals court —

132
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:22,860
the Ninth Circuit —

133
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:26,880
looked at a case where officers took
a man's phone and simply pressed

134
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:28,120
his own thumb to the sensor

135
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,020
to unlock it.

136
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:32,033
The court said that did not
violate the Fifth Amendment.

137
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:33,964
Pressing a thumb, they reasoned,

138
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:35,323
takes no thought at all.

139
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:37,528
His body opened the door against him

140
00:04:37,558 --> 00:04:37,858
—

141
00:04:37,859 --> 00:04:39,860
and the Constitution had nothing to say

142
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:40,710
about it.

143
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,040
But even that ruling came

144
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:43,724
with a chilling little footnote.

145
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:46,120
The court hinted the answer might have

146
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,340
been different if the officers had made

147
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:50,400
the man choose which finger to use

148
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:51,465
—

149
00:04:51,466 --> 00:04:53,010
because choosing is a decision,

150
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:55,040
and a decision happens in your mind.

151
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,680
Sit with how thin that line is.

152
00:04:58,060 --> 00:04:59,797
A thumb they press to the sensor:

153
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:01,380
no protection.

154
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:02,720
The very same thumb,

155
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:05,380
if you are the one who decides to use it:

156
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:06,400
maybe protected.

157
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:11,129
Whether the Fifth Amendment shields you can
come down to a single flicker of choice —

158
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:15,060
the hair's breadth between your body doing
something, and your mind deciding it.

159
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:16,816
Sit with what that means.

160
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,187
The passcode you can refuse to speak.

161
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,280
But the fingerprint sensor you set

162
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,682
up for convenience —

163
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:24,380
the Face ID that unlocks your phone

164
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:27,143
before you've even thought about it —

165
00:05:27,144 --> 00:05:31,280
those may hand the police everything, precisely
because they ask nothing of your mind.

166
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:32,520
So it's settled.

167
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:34,283
Your mind is a fortress;

168
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:35,728
your body is an open door.

169
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:38,180
Clean, simple, done.

170
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:39,222
Except —

171
00:05:39,223 --> 00:05:40,600
it isn't.

172
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:42,334
Because barely a year later,

173
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:45,600
another federal court looked
at almost the exact same act,

174
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,540
and came to the opposite conclusion.

175
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:49,860
In 2025, the D.C.

176
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:51,380
Circuit ruled that forcing a man

177
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:55,798
to unlock his phone with his fingerprint
did violate the Fifth Amendment —

178
00:05:55,799 --> 00:05:59,430
because obeying that command, the court
said, quietly admits something you know:

179
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:00,500
that it's your phone,

180
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,060
and that you know how to open

181
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:02,670
it.

182
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:03,250
Same act.

183
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,180
Same country.

184
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:05,220
Opposite answer.

185
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:07,012
And it isn't just fingerprints.

186
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:08,724
On the passcode —

187
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:12,157
the thing that's supposed to be the safe part —

188
00:06:12,158 --> 00:06:13,530
the states can't agree either.

189
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,040
In Pennsylvania, in Indiana,

190
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,480
in Utah, top courts have ruled

191
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:18,860
you cannot be forced to give

192
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:19,590
it up.

193
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:21,041
In New Jersey, in Illinois,

194
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:22,788
courts have ruled you can.

195
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:24,378
The same locked phone,

196
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,231
the same Constitution —

197
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:28,940
and your right depends on which side

198
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:30,782
of a state line you're standing on.

199
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,740
And this has already happened to real

200
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:33,480
people.

201
00:06:33,510 --> 00:06:34,740
A man in New Jersey,

202
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:36,320
ordered by a court to surrender

203
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:37,340
his passcodes.

204
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:39,321
A man in the Ninth Circuit,

205
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,040
whose own thumb was pressed

206
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:42,520
to his phone at the side

207
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:43,420
of a road.

208
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:44,480
Same country.

209
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:45,727
Same Constitution.

210
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:47,580
Opposite outcomes —

211
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:49,680
decided not by what they did,

212
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:51,500
but by which courthouse happened

213
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:52,658
to be looking at them.

214
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:54,820
How can courts looking at the same

215
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,340
words land so far apart?

216
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,340
It comes down to a single,

217
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:01,160
almost philosophical question the lawyers call

218
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:02,440
the foregone conclusion.

219
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:04,190
Roughly:

220
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:05,900
the government can force you to hand

221
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:07,960
something over if it already knows,

222
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:10,100
for certain, what it's getting.

223
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:13,040
And here is the whole fight, in one sentence.

224
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:16,226
Does the government already 'know' your passcode

225
00:07:16,745 --> 00:07:17,045
—

226
00:07:17,046 --> 00:07:18,940
the fact that it exists, that it's yours?

227
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:22,000
Then handing it over reveals nothing new,

228
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,460
and you can be forced.

229
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:25,720
Or is the thing it really wants

230
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:28,120
the ocean of secrets behind the passcode

231
00:07:29,248 --> 00:07:29,548
—

232
00:07:29,549 --> 00:07:31,170
which it obviously doesn't know?

233
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:32,500
Then forcing you to open

234
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,200
it is forcing you to testify.

235
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:36,780
Courts split on which of those two

236
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:38,569
things the question is even about.

237
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:40,917
And on that hair-thin difference,

238
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:42,860
your privacy stands or falls.

239
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,260
And there is one place where even

240
00:07:45,260 --> 00:07:47,523
this fragile protection gets thinner still:

241
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:48,740
the border.

242
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,000
At an airport, or a crossing,

243
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:52,640
the normal rules bend.

244
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,160
For years the government has claimed

245
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:57,783
it can search your phone
there with no warrant at all —

246
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:01,213
and the courts cannot agree
on how far that power goes.

247
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:03,920
Some say officers need at least

248
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:05,240
a reason to suspect you.

249
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:07,286
Others say they need nothing.

250
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,900
In 2023, one federal judge became

251
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:13,911
the first to rule that the government does need
a warrant to search a phone at the border —

252
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:15,400
a real crack in the wall.

253
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:17,260
But that too is unsettled,

254
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:19,200
and it binds almost no one yet.

255
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:20,780
So the same device

256
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:22,380
that is a fortress in your living

257
00:08:22,380 --> 00:08:24,100
room can become an open book

258
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:25,180
the moment you land.

259
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:26,690
Airports.

260
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:27,620
Traffic stops.

261
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:28,860
State lines.

262
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:31,027
Every one of them a different answer.

263
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:33,391
You would think, with this much confusion,

264
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:35,980
someone at the very top would finally step in.

265
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:38,020
You would think, with the country

266
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:40,600
this divided, the Supreme Court would step

267
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:41,500
in and settle it.

268
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:42,860
It hasn't.

269
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:44,980
Case after case has climbed all

270
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:46,252
the way up —

271
00:08:46,253 --> 00:08:48,147
Pennsylvania, New Jersey, Illinois —

272
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:51,060
asking the justices to finally draw

273
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:51,930
the line.

274
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:53,840
And three times, the Court has quietly

275
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:54,740
turned them away.

276
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:59,100
It has never decided whether you can
be forced to unlock your phone at all.

277
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:00,780
So there is no national rule.

278
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:02,990
There is a patchwork —

279
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:04,780
a map where the same phone,

280
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:06,140
in the same hand,

281
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:09,100
is protected in one state and pried

282
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:10,000
open in the next.

283
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:12,320
A right that changes when you cross

284
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,660
a bridge isn't really a settled right

285
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:15,550
at all.

286
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:17,380
It's a coin flip you don't get

287
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:18,280
to see coming.

288
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:21,153
So where does that leave you —

289
00:09:21,154 --> 00:09:22,530
tonight, at that car window?

290
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:25,100
With one uncomfortable, defensible truth.

291
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:27,664
In a lot of American courtrooms,

292
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,080
the passcode you keep in your head
is better protected than the face

293
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:33,300
on the front of it or the thumb on your hand.

294
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:34,340
Not everywhere.

295
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:35,540
Not guaranteed.

296
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:37,231
But as a rule of thumb,

297
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,720
your mind is the strongest lock you own.

298
00:09:40,020 --> 00:09:43,720
And here is the part that puts a
little of the power back in your hands.

299
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:44,980
On most modern phones there

300
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:47,680
is a simple line between the easy

301
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:49,580
lock and the strong one.

302
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:51,520
Power the phone all the way off.

303
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:52,740
When it turns back on,

304
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:55,261
it will not take your face,

305
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:57,680
and it will not take your thumb

306
00:09:57,684 --> 00:09:57,984
—

307
00:09:57,985 --> 00:10:01,600
not until the passcode has been typed

308
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:03,720
once. In that single moment,

309
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:06,280
the convenience turns back into the combination.

310
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:08,780
The lock moves back inside your mind

311
00:10:08,781 --> 00:10:09,281
—

312
00:10:09,282 --> 00:10:11,986
the one place that, for now, is the
hardest for anyone to force open.

313
00:10:11,086 --> 00:10:15,480
Which flips something you do every day on its
head. That fingerprint, that face-scan you set

314
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,112
up because it was fast and easy

315
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:18,042
—

316
00:10:18,043 --> 00:10:19,060
in a courtroom, it may

317
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:20,797
be the weakest lock on the phone.

318
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:23,087
The convenience is the vulnerability.

319
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,880
The thing that opens your phone without
a thought may open it for someone

320
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:28,260
else without your consent.

321
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:30,428
Remember how this started.

322
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:32,068
An officer at your window,

323
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,195
your whole life glowing in his hand.

324
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:35,811
He can take your thumb.

325
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:37,198
He can take your face.

326
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:38,950
Your body, it turns out,

327
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:40,348
can be turned against you.

328
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:41,989
But your mind —

329
00:10:41,990 --> 00:10:43,832
the passcode you never say out loud

330
00:10:44,264 --> 00:10:44,564
—

331
00:10:44,565 --> 00:10:46,650
may be the last thing they can't force.

332
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:47,580
For now.

333
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:49,095
For now —

334
00:10:49,096 --> 00:10:52,360
because the case that finally
settles this hasn't been decided yet.

335
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:55,720
Somewhere, right now, there's a traffic stop,

336
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:58,580
a locked phone, a demand to open

337
00:10:58,580 --> 00:10:59,480
it.

338
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:01,560
One of those ordinary moments will become

339
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:02,940
the case that draws the line

340
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:03,840
for everyone.

341
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:05,520
And there is nothing that says

342
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:06,467
it won't be yours.

343
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:08,700
We built machines that read our faces

344
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:10,580
and our fingerprints to make our lives

345
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:11,480
easier.

346
00:11:11,580 --> 00:11:13,440
We never quite decided what happens

347
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:15,580
when someone else holds them up to the lock.

348
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:17,320
Until we do —

349
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:18,980
until a court finally says

350
00:11:18,980 --> 00:11:20,182
where the line is —

351
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:22,380
the only key they may never

352
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:23,080
be able to take

353
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:24,580
from you is the one you never

354
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:25,480
speak.

355
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:27,400
If you just learned your passcode may

356
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:29,719
protect you more than your face —

357
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:31,200
hit like, so more people know

358
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:33,720
it before an officer is ever holding their phone.
